Searching
Searching means finding a particular item in a collection of data.
Example: Finding the number 7 in [2, 5, 7, 10].
There are two important searching techniques:
Linear Search – checks elements one by one.
Binary Search – repeatedly divides the search area into half.

Sorting 
means arranging data in a particular order, usually ascending or descending.
Example: [8, 3, 5, 1] sorted in ascending order becomes [1, 3, 5, 8].
Common methods include bubble sort, selection sort, insertion sort, merge sort, and quick sort.
Common Sorting Algorithms
Bubble Sort – repeatedly compares and swaps adjacent elements.
Selection Sort – repeatedly selects the smallest/largest element.
Insertion Sort – inserts each element into its correct position.
